  <h2 class="section-title">Social Security Coverage</h2>
  <p>The services you perform in the exercise of your ministry or religious vocation may be subject to social security and Medicare taxes. The rules for taxing these earnings are complex. For more information, see <span data-field="section_ref">{{ section_ref }}</span>.</p>
  <p>You are a member of a religious order who has taken a vow of poverty. You are exempt from SE tax and FICA tax. For more information, see <span data-field="section_ref">{{ section_ref }}</span>.</p>
  <p>You are a member of a religious order who has not taken a vow of poverty. You may be subject to SE tax and FICA tax. For more information, see <span data-field="section_ref">{{ section_ref }}</span>.</p>
  <p>If you are a minister of a church, your earnings for the services you perform in your capacity as a minister are generally not subject to SE tax. However, you may be subject to FICA tax. For more information, see <span data-field="section_ref">{{ section_ref }}</span>.</p>

  <h3 class="subsection-title">Ministers Defined</h3>
  <p>Ministers are individuals who are duly ordained, licensed, or recognized by a church or denomination. They perform religious services and are generally exempt from SE tax. However, they may be subject to FICA tax.</p>

  <h3 class="subsection-title">Religious Workers</h3>
  <p>Religious workers are individuals who perform services for a religious organization. They may be subject to SE tax and FICA tax, depending on their status.</p>

  <h3 class="subsection-title">Members of Religious Orders</h3>
  <p>Members of religious orders are individuals who belong to a religious order. Some members are exempt from taxes, while others may be subject to taxes.</p>

  <h3 class="subsection-title">Christian Science Practitioners and Readers</h3>
  <p>Christian Science practitioners and readers are generally exempt from social security and Medicare taxes. For more information, see <span data-field="section_ref">{{ section_ref }}</span>.</p>

  <h3 class="subsection-title">Figuring Net Earnings From Self-Employment for SE Tax</h3>
  <p>There are two methods for figuring your net earnings from self-employment for SE tax: the net earnings method and the gross income method.</p>
  <p><strong>Regular method.</strong></p>
  <p>Use the regular method. Under this method, you subtract your deductible expenses from your gross income to arrive at your net earnings from self-employment. You must also subtract one-half of your self-employment tax to arrive at your net earnings for SE tax purposes.</p>
  <p><strong>Nonfarm optional method.</strong></p>
  <p>If you are an employee of a church or religious organization, you may be able to use the nonfarm optional method. This method allows you to calculate your net earnings from self-employment differently.</p>
</div>

