  <h2 class="section-title">Qualified Services</h2>
  <p>Qualified services, in general, are the services you perform in the exercise of your ministry or religious vocation. These services are generally not subject to SE tax. However, you may be subject to FICA tax.</p>
  <p>The following discussions provide more detail on the types of services that are considered qualified services for members of the clergy and religious workers.</p>

  <h3 class="subsection-title">Ministers</h3>
  <p>Ministers perform services as a minister, priest, rabbi, etc. These services are generally not subject to SE tax.</p>

  <h3 class="subsection-title">Religious Workers</h3>
  <p>Religious workers perform services for a religious organization. They may be subject to SE tax and FICA tax, depending on their status.</p>

  <h3 class="subsection-title">Members of Religious Orders</h3>
  <p>Members of religious orders perform services in the exercise of duties required by the order. Some members are exempt from taxes, while others may be subject to taxes.</p>

  <h3 class="subsection-title">Christian Science Practitioners and Readers</h3>
  <p>Christian Science practitioners and readers perform services in the exercise of their religious vocation. These services are generally not subject to SE tax.</p>

  <h3 class="subsection-title">Figuring Net Earnings From Self-Employment for SE Tax</h3>
  <p>There are two methods for figuring your net earnings from self-employment for SE tax: the net earnings method and the gross income method.</p>
  <p><strong>Regular method.</strong></p>
  <p>Use the regular method. Under this method, you subtract your deductible expenses from your gross income to arrive at your net earnings from self-employment. You must also subtract one-half of your self-employment tax to arrive at your net earnings for SE tax purposes.</p>
  <p><strong>Nonfarm optional method.</strong></p>
  <p>If you are an employee of a church or religious organization, you may be able to use the nonfarm optional method. This method allows you to calculate your net earnings from self-employment differently.</p>
</div>

