  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; font-weight: bold;">You perform qualified services at churches located away from your community.</h2>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">You actually use the rental allowance to meet your payment home.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Cantors. If you have a bona fide commission and your congregation is a non-profit, you can use the rental allowance to perform substantially all the religious functions of the Jewish faith, you can deduct a rental allowance from your gross income.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; font-weight: bold;">Expenses Allocable to Tax-Free Income</h2>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you receive a rental or parsonage allowance as compensation for services performed, you must allocate a portion of the expenses of operating your ministry to your tax-free income. You can deduct the portion of the expenses that is allocated to your tax-free rental or parsonage allowance.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Exception. This rule does not apply to any income that is not derived from interest or real estate taxes on your home.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; font-weight: bold;">Pay—Members of Religious Organizations</h2>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Your pay may be exempt from both income tax and SE tax. If you are a member of a religious order who:</p>
    <ul style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 5px;">
      <li>Has taken a vow of poverty.</li>
      <li>Is paid solely for services performed as an agent of the order and in the exercise of duties required by the order, and</li>
      <li>Renounces the pay and gives it to the order.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; font-weight: bold;">Health Insurance Costs of Self-Employed Ministers</h2>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you are self-employed, you may be able to deduct the cost of health insurance premiums for yourself, your spouse, and your dependents. You can deduct the premiums if you meet certain requirements.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you qualify, you can take this deduction as an adjustment to income. You must meet the requirements in the instructions for Form 1040 to use this deduction.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">The expenses taken into account for pur-poses of this deduction are not allowed as medical expense deductions on Schedule A.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; font-weight: bold;">Foreign Earned Income</h2>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Certain income may be exempt from income tax if you meet the requirements of the Foreign Earned Income Exclusion. Publication 54 discusses the requirements for the Foreign Earned Income Exclusion. Publication 54 also discusses the requirements for taxpayers with income from U.S. possessions.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; font-weight: bold;">Expense Items</h2>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">The tax treatment of ministerial or trade or busi-ness expenses depends on the nature of the income, and health insurance costs is discussed in Publication 517.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; font-weight: bold;">Earned Income Credit</h2>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">The earned income credit is a special credit for certain low-to-moderate-income workers. You may be eligible for this credit if you meet certain requirements.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">You can take the credit if your earned income is less than a certain amount and you meet other requirements.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; font-weight: bold;">W-2 Wage and Tax Statement</h2>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">The W-2 Wage and Tax Statement is a form that employers use to report wages and the amount of taxes withheld from an employee's pay. It is used by the employee to file their income tax return.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; font-weight: bold;">How to Get Tax Help</h2>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">You can get help with untaxed tax issues from various sources. You can contact the IRS directly or use other resources.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">The Taxpayer Advocate independently rep-resents taxpayers and can help resolve tax problems that have not been fixed through normal IRS channels.</p>
  </div>
</div>

